the specified architectural pattern.\n\nExplicitly cast the 21 columns to their correct datatypes for data integrity. String-type columns will be cast directly. Numeric types will be handled via `pd.to_numeric` with `errors=\"coerce\"`. Date columns will be\n<truncated 4955 bytes>\n within rows not present in `geo_correctos`. The code will be designed to accept either explicit count parameters or infer these counts from intermediate Parquet files, with a fallback mechanism for seamless unit testing.\n\n\nInitial focus is on implementing the diagnostic map as specified, targeting publication quality for thesis use. A non-interactive backend is essential for unattended execution using the \"Agg\" backend to ensure headless operation before importing `matplotlib.pyplot`.\n\n\nThe diagnostic map utilizes a non-interactive backend for headless execution, crucial for unattended environments. The approach involves direct control with `Figure` and `plt.subplots`, paired with `plt.close(fig)` to prevent display pop-ups. Using `matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")` ensures compatibility across various environments.\n\nThe map displays two layers: cantons and provinces. Cantons are rendered in a light gray color with thin white borders. Province boundaries are represented by a darker gray line, derived by dissolving the canton polygons based on provincial divisions. This produces a clear visual hierarchy.\n\n\nThe diagnostic map visualizes continental Ecuador, showcasing provinces and cantonal subdivisions. A point layer of 38,565 data points highlights key urban concentrations like Guayaquil and Quito. A micro-jitter (0.004 degrees, approximately 440 meters) is applied for visual clarity using a deterministic seed to prevent overplotting of events at the same coordinate. This jittered point data, symbolized in a soft red hue, is overlaid onto the map.\n\n\nThe map's design must be optimized for a formal thesis with a white background and sub